WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.810
The Bitcoin 2021 Conference in Miami.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.940
Wasn't just a celebration
of the end of the pandemic

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.760
and an opportunity to
gather up close and maskless

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.960
with more than 10,000
ideological fellow travelers,

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.250
it was also a watershed
moment for a technological

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.237
and cultural movement

00:00:22.237 --> 00:00:24.270
whose goal is nothing short

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.530
of the separation of money and state.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.720
Bitcoin emerged just a dozen years ago,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.590
when a pseudonymous genius
shared a nine page paper

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.370
on an obscure email list.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.620
And now it's the third largest
currency on the planet.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.830
In another dozen years, we
may look back on Bitcoin 2021

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.570
as the Woodstock of the crypto generation.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.610
The headline acts included
former Congressman Ron Paul.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.810
I admit there's a lot of
the things about Bitcoin

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.260
and other things that I
don't fully understand,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.840
but I understand liberty

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.250
Twitter and Square CEO, Jack Dorsey.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.850
We need to be able to transact
with this every single day

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.046
and everyone around the world

00:01:02.046 --> 00:01:03.615
needs to transact with it everyday.

00:01:03.615 --> 00:01:04.448
Skateboard legend, Tony Hawk,

00:01:04.448 --> 00:01:05.950
boxer, Floyd Mayweather,

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.270
sex tape, entrepreneur, Paris Hilton,

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.380
DJ and record producer Diplo

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.210
and Ross Ulbricht who beamed
in from federal prison,

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.560
where he is serving an
effective life sentence

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.997
for creating the pioneering dark website

00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:22.750
"Silk Road" which helped bootstrap
Bitcoin it's early years.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.680
The conference ended with an announcement

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.750
that El Salvador will become
the first country on earth

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.253
to recognize Bitcoin as legal tender.

00:01:30.253 --> 00:01:32.123
I will send to Congress
a bill that will make

00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:34.150
Bitcoin a legal tender in El Salvador.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.110
One of the breakout
stars of the conference

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.510
was Cynthia Lummis a 66-year
old freshmen Republican Senator

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.343
from Wyoming.

00:01:41.343 --> 00:01:43.890
From the stage and in an
interview with Reason,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.950
Lummis forcefully made
the case that Bitcoin

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.780
not only provides a fully
legitimate alternative

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.220
store of value and medium of exchange,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.740
but would act as a
check on the devaluation

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.160
of the U.S. dollar and other currencies

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.480
through the runaway
creation of fiat money.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.050
She also believes that
the growth of Bitcoin,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.322
assailed for its heavy use of electricity,

00:02:04.322 --> 00:02:07.240
is acting as a spur to
create renewable energy

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.050
in Wyoming and around the globe.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.400
And she extolled its privacy features

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.750
in a world of increasing surveillance

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.700
by governments and corporations alike.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.520
Senator, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.680
It's my pleasure, Nick.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.210
What's your main interest in crypto?

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.710
I mean you're in the Senate
and in Congress more broadly.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.730
You're like a leading light
on this type of stuff.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.520
What do you see as the value
of this new technology?

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.010
Especially as it intersects

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.620
with a kind of fiat currency system

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.453
that has really gone off the rails.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.310
I had been my state's
treasurer, in Wyoming.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.640
I think that suffices,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.500
I understand why you're
interest in crypto.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.600
Yeah, I was always looking
for a store of value

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.808
and I see Bitcoin, specifically,
as a great store of value.

00:02:51.808 --> 00:02:56.390
Only 21 million will ever be developed.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:00.750
And so we know that scarcity
will protect its value

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.550
going into the future,
unlike the U.S. dollar

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.820
that we're printing more
of every single day.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.021
Oh yeah.

00:03:07.021 --> 00:03:08.370
I mean, by the end of this interview

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.548
there'll be millions more
in circulation, right?

00:03:10.548 --> 00:03:14.410
But I would think, you're
in the U.S. Senate,

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.150
is Bitcoin or is crypto more broadly,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.074
is that a competitor to the U.S. dollar?

00:03:20.074 --> 00:03:22.040
It's not a competitor

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.809
but it is an alternative saving instrument

00:03:24.809 --> 00:03:26.361
for people like me,

00:03:26.361 --> 00:03:30.129
who are concerned that
the U.S. dollar saved,

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:35.129
will be worth far less than
when it's deposited in savings.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.430
So to me, I see it as an
opportunity to save for my future

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.280
in something that will grow
in value, not fall in value.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.425
Do you own Bitcoin or other crypto?

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:46.780
I do. I do.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.870
Okay. Are you cashing out?

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.780
Oh no.
You're long on Bitcoin.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.366
I'm what they call a Hodler. I'm long.

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.160
I'm in it for the distance.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.840
You are a Hodler.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.410
You are working both to bring
kind of an understanding

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.260
and a legislative agenda
around crypto into fruition.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.307
What's the regulatory framework
that you want to see crypto

00:04:08.307 --> 00:04:10.769
being used that apart from,

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:13.846
currently it basically
is treated as a private

00:04:13.846 --> 00:04:16.830
commodity.
Commodity. Mm-hm.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.990
So it's subject to things
like capital gains tax.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.400
Where do you see things going?

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.693
What your dream vision?

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.139
It is to create a regulatory
and statutory framework.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:32.139
A sandbox if you will, that
innovators know the parameters

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:38.510
but the parameters don't
constrain them in their innovation

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.150
as they develop new uses,
new tools, new opportunities.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:46.810
So the blockchain will
provide, for example,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.980
the opportunity to put both contracts

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.000
and the money or value
associated with that contract

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.500
on the blockchain.

